  "title": "Great Migration of African Americans",
  "summary": "From about 1916 to 1970 more than 6 million Black Americans left the rural South for cities of the North, Midwest and West. They were pushed by limited economic opportunity and by Jim Crow laws and violence, and pulled by industrial jobs opened in World War I when European immigration fell. Destinations included New York, Chicago, Detroit and Pittsburgh.",
